---
import DerelictConstruction from "../../../components/studies/DerelictConstruction.astro";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((entry) => entry.slug === "derelicts-2026-11-06");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <aside aria-label="Study outcome">
    <h2>Stopped / Not doable in this cycle</h2>
    <p>
      This design loop failed. It ended on 6 November 2026 after 02.3 without an
      accepted derelict design. The retained sketches document the attempt; they
      are not an approved direction. Readiness and implementation scope were not
      reached. This outcome does not establish that derelicts are infeasible as
      a game feature.
    </p>
  </aside>
  <section>
    <h2>01 / Something broken, still carrying energy</h2>
    <p>
      A derelict is a remnant of a broken megastructure, sent toward known
      systems. Its passage through the solar system runs from border to border:
      the player intercepts something already on a journey. Destroying it
      creates physical splinters and releases an energy fragment for the player
      to chase. The encounter moves from recognition, through rupture, into a
      pursuit through dangerous debris.
    </p>
    <p>
      The test-flight trial supplies the starting interaction: a passing wreck
      breaks into four damaging fragments and one drifting energy pickup. Its
      current near-ship spawning and finite lifetime do not yet establish a full
      border-to-border pass. The study develops the object's identity and that
      encounter's legibility together. A promising character is a vast structure
      reduced to one stubborn, coherent remnant: broken edges imply its former
      scale, while surviving connections make its eventual rupture meaningful.
    </p>
    <p>
      The supplied Split Spine reference offers a distinct material palette,
      long angular masses and readable planes. Its pieces are too disconnected
      for the intact derelict, and its bright central crystal does not settle
      the energy fragment's form. This cycle asks what connected construction,
      material language and release sequence make the remnant, hazardous
      splinters and desirable energy read clearly at flight scale. The
      fragment's appearance and visibility before rupture remain open. The
      sender, original megastructure's purpose, reward balance and wider
      progression remain adjacent questions rather than prerequisites.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/studies/derelicts-2026-11-06/reference.png"
        alt="Supplied Split Spine inspiration: long faceted grey masses separated around a speculative glowing crystal, with silhouette thumbnails and a small flight-scale view."
        width="480"
        height="738"
      >
      <figcaption>
        Supplied visual reference. Material and shape inspiration; the separated
        construction and crystal are unresolved.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>
  <details id="construction">
    <summary>02.1 / Blade language and depth</summary>
    <p>
      The reference’s long, almost blade-like parts are a quality to retain.
      Connections and the background beneath the energy need a separate, much
      darker value range, below both the outer shapes and the fragment.
    </p>
  </details>
  <section id="blades">
    <h2>02.2 / Geometry needs a character</h2>
    <p>
      Long, tapered shapes and a dark connecting bed did not establish what the
      object was. The construction read as deliberately arranged blades. Shape
      words alone were insufficient: the next proposal starts with the nature of
      the original structure and the damage that produced the remnant.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="torn-structure">
    <h2>02.3 / Built before it was broken</h2>
    <p class="character">
      <strong
        >A torn piece of something immense, still held together by a structure
        that has survived its purpose.</strong
      >
    </p>
    <p>
      The desired feeling is monumental, wounded and inert, with something still
      active inside. The sharpness comes from long fractures through structural
      material. Repeated sections and uninterrupted planes recall its
      construction; unequal ends and peeled layers describe what happened to it.
    </p>
    <div class="hero" id="remnant-portrait">
      <DerelictConstruction />
      <div class="scale">
        <DerelictConstruction small />
        <span>Approx. 36 px long · static scale probe</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="comparison" id="structure-reasoning">
      <article>
        <h3>Before: a continuous structural member</h3>
        <DerelictConstruction reconstruction />
        <p>
          Dashed continuations propose parallel ribs running far beyond this
          piece. The repeated cross-section gives the long planes a structural
          reason. This is a construction hypothesis, not settled megastructure
          lore.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>After: the tear makes the points</h3>
        <p>
          One rib stays long and largely whole. A second tears diagonally along
          its length; a thin lower layer peels away. The other ends retain
          blunt, broken sections. The result has no matching set of weapon tips
          and no purposeful nose.
        </p>
        <p>
          A continuous dark body sits underneath. It is the surviving depth of
          the structure, rather than a set of added brackets. Its recessed
          interior reserves darkness for the energy fragment, whose form remains
          open.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p>
      This round tests whether construction and damage communicate the agreed
      character. The exact form remains provisional. Ancient age is a story
      direction, not something these flat planes alone establish. No flight,
      collision or breakup behavior is demonstrated here; destruction still
      creates splinters and releases the energy fragment for pursuit.
    </p>
  </section>
</StudyLayout>

<style>
.hero {
  max-width: 900px;
  margin: var(--spacing-lg) auto;
}
.comparison {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
  gap: var(--spacing-xl);
  margin-block: var(--spacing-xl);
}
article {
  padding: var(--spacing-lg);
  border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
}
article p {
  font-size: var(--font-size-body);
}
.scale {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: var(--spacing-lg);
  min-height: 64px;
  color: var(--color-text-muted);
}
figure {
  margin-block: var(--spacing-xl);
}
img {
  display: block;
  max-width: 100%;
  height: auto;
}
figcaption {
  max-width: 65ch;
  margin-top: var(--spacing-md);
  color: var(--color-text-muted);
}
</style>
